1
00:01:40,363 --> 00:01:42,114
My name is Kathy H.

2
00:01:43,408 --> 00:01:45,284
I'm 28 years old.

3
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:50,998
I've been a carer
for nine years.

4
00:01:58,381 --> 00:02:00,340
And I'm good at my job.

5
00:02:01,176 --> 00:02:03,969
My patients
always do better
than expected

6
00:02:04,053 --> 00:02:06,054
and are hardly
ever classified
as agitated,

7
00:02:07,557 --> 00:02:10,350
even if they're
about to make a donation.

8
00:02:13,354 --> 00:02:15,314
I'm not trying to boast,

9
00:02:15,398 --> 00:02:18,525
but I feel a great sense
of pride in what we do.

10
00:02:19,527 --> 00:02:22,237
Carers and donors
have achieved so much.

11
00:02:25,617 --> 00:02:27,868
That said,
we aren't machines.

12
00:02:31,414 --> 00:02:33,624
In the end,
it wears you down.

13
00:02:35,418 --> 00:02:39,880
I suppose that's why
I now spend most of my time
not looking forwards

14
00:02:39,964 --> 00:02:42,132
but looking back,

15
00:02:42,217 --> 00:02:45,427
to The Cottages and Hailsham,

16
00:02:46,179 --> 00:02:47,930
and what happened to us there.

17
00:02:49,432 --> 00:02:50,849
Me,

18
00:02:52,185 --> 00:02:54,102
Tommy,

19
00:02:54,771 --> 00:02:57,022
and Ruth.

20
00:02:57,440 --> 00:02:59,107
(BIRDS TWITTERING)

21
00:03:00,944 --> 00:03:02,486
(ORGAN PLAYING)

22
00:03:06,074 --> 00:03:11,662
ALL: (SINGING)
When we are scattered
afar and asunder

23
00:03:11,746 --> 00:03:16,500
Parted are those
who are singing today

24
00:03:16,918 --> 00:03:22,089
When we look back
and forgetfully wonder

25
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:27,135
What we were like
in our learning and play

26
00:03:27,762 --> 00:03:32,766
Oh, the great days
will bring distance enchanted

27
00:03:33,131 --> 00:03:37,854
Days of fresh air
in the rain
and the sun

28
00:03:38,106 --> 00:03:43,151
How we rejoiced
as we struggled
and panted

29
00:03:43,319 --> 00:03:50,158
Echoes of dreamland,
Hailsham lives on

30
00:03:57,083 --> 00:03:58,709
Good morning, students.

31
00:03:58,793 --> 00:04:01,670
CHILDREN:
Good morning,
Miss Emily.

32
00:04:02,797 --> 00:04:06,925
It has come to my attention
that three burned cigarettes
were discovered,

33
00:04:07,302 --> 00:04:10,387
hidden behind a pot
in the flower garden.

34
00:04:12,682 --> 00:04:14,850
I know that on occasion
students have seen

35
00:04:14,934 --> 00:04:18,729
some of the caretakers
and deliverymen
smoking cigarettes,

36
00:04:18,813 --> 00:04:23,275
but I must emphasize,
once again,
that it is much, much worse

37
00:04:23,359 --> 00:04:26,862
for a student of Hailsham
to smoke cigarettes
than anyone else.

38
00:04:29,240 --> 00:04:32,617
Students of Hailsham
are special.

39
00:04:34,537 --> 00:04:38,707
Keeping yourselves well,
keeping yourselves
healthy inside,

40
00:04:38,791 --> 00:04:40,959
is of paramount importance.

41
00:04:41,294 --> 00:04:43,253
Have I made myself clear?

42
00:04:43,338 --> 00:04:45,464
CHILDREN: Yes, Miss Emily.

43
00:04:48,259 --> 00:04:51,720
RUTH: I think, one day,
I'd like five horses.

44
00:04:52,764 --> 00:04:57,476
I don't know all their
names, but I know
I'd like five horses.

45
00:04:57,602 --> 00:04:59,311
The best
would be Thunder.

46
00:04:59,395 --> 00:05:02,564
He'd be dangerous to ride,
so you can't ride him.

47
00:05:03,649 --> 00:05:05,734
You can ride

48
00:05:06,694 --> 00:05:08,320
Bramble.

49
00:05:09,072 --> 00:05:12,157
Just so long as you don't
use your crop on him.

50
00:05:13,785 --> 00:05:15,827
That's pretty.

51
00:05:15,912 --> 00:05:17,329
You have it.

52
00:05:17,413 --> 00:05:18,747
(CHUCKLES)

53
00:05:19,999 --> 00:05:22,918
Look. Who's that
with Miss Emily?

54
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:26,588
Do you think
it's the new guardian?

55
00:05:26,672 --> 00:05:27,839
KATHY: Miss Lucy.

56
00:05:29,384 --> 00:05:31,301
I think it must be.

57
00:05:31,386 --> 00:05:34,012
We haven't had
a new guardian for ages.

58
00:05:34,597 --> 00:05:36,348
(CHILDREN CHEERING)

59
00:05:46,443 --> 00:05:49,112
CHILDREN: (CHANTING)
Arthur! Arthur!

60
00:05:53,033 --> 00:05:54,534
Catch it, Tommy.

61
00:06:09,299 --> 00:06:10,675
(LAUGHING)

62
00:06:14,596 --> 00:06:16,389
(GIRLS CHATTERING
INDISTINCTLY)

63
00:06:17,266 --> 00:06:19,851
Why didn't Tommy
get the ball?

64
00:06:21,603 --> 00:06:23,229
Excuse me, Miss Lucy?

65
00:06:23,647 --> 00:06:25,898
Why didn't Tommy
get the ball?

66
00:06:26,733 --> 00:06:27,942
When it bounced
over the fence,

67
00:06:28,026 --> 00:06:29,402
it couldn't have
been far from him.

68
00:06:30,070 --> 00:06:32,447
The fence is the boundary
of the Hailsham grounds.

69
00:06:32,573 --> 00:06:35,241
We don't go outside
the boundary, Miss Lucy.

70
00:06:35,492 --> 00:06:37,243
It's too dangerous.

71
00:06:37,703 --> 00:06:39,162
Dangerous?

72
00:06:39,246 --> 00:06:41,873
RUTH: There was once
a boy who had a big row
with all his friends

73
00:06:41,957 --> 00:06:44,333
and then ran off
beyond the boundary.

74
00:06:44,418 --> 00:06:48,629
They found him,
two days later, in the woods.
Tied to a tree.

75
00:06:49,173 --> 00:06:50,756
With his hands
and feet cut off.

76
00:06:51,550 --> 00:06:52,800
Dead.

77
00:06:53,594 --> 00:06:55,553
RUTH: And there was the girl
who climbed over the fence,

78
00:06:55,637 --> 00:06:56,971
by the front gate.

79
00:06:57,055 --> 00:07:00,308
And when she tried
to get back,
she wasn't allowed.

80
00:07:00,601 --> 00:07:03,227
And then she starved.
Out there.

81
00:07:03,937 --> 00:07:05,688
Right by the gates.

82
00:07:08,442 --> 00:07:09,609
Who told you
these stories?

83
00:07:10,152 --> 00:07:12,195
RUTH: Everybody knows them.

84
00:07:12,446 --> 00:07:14,113
And how do you know
they're true?

85
00:07:14,448 --> 00:07:15,448
Of course
they're true.

86
00:07:16,033 --> 00:07:18,826
Who'd make up stories
as horrible as that?

87
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:21,913
(SCHOOL BELL RINGING)

88
00:07:26,251 --> 00:07:28,628
(CHILDREN CHATTERING
INDISTINCTLY)

89
00:07:45,562 --> 00:07:48,356
Tommy, what are you doing?

90
00:07:48,649 --> 00:07:50,775
What is it, exactly?

91
00:07:50,859 --> 00:07:53,653
I think it must be a dog.
Is it a dog, Tommy?

92
00:07:54,029 --> 00:07:56,572
ARTHUR: It can't be a dog.
lts eyes are too small.

93
00:07:56,782 --> 00:07:59,158
I think it must be
a kind of rat.

94
00:07:59,243 --> 00:08:01,035
Yes, that's it.
Tommy's drawn a rat.

95
00:08:01,119 --> 00:08:02,203
It's not finished yet.

96
00:08:02,287 --> 00:08:04,288
That's right.
You haven't finished
the whiskers.

97
00:08:04,373 --> 00:08:06,332
Do you think Tommy's rat
will be selected
for The Gallery?

98
00:08:06,416 --> 00:08:07,959
TOMMY: It's not a rat.

99
00:08:10,712 --> 00:08:12,338
It's an elephant.

100
00:08:12,464 --> 00:08:14,340
GERALDINE: Children,
back to work.

101
00:08:23,475 --> 00:08:25,309
(DEVICE BEEPS)

102
00:08:27,646 --> 00:08:29,355
(CHILDREN LAUGHING)

103
00:08:34,027 --> 00:08:36,571
(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)

104
00:08:44,454 --> 00:08:47,582
He's got his shirt on.
His favorite polo shirt.

105
00:08:48,834 --> 00:08:51,210
RUTH: He really doesn't
suspect a thing.

106
00:08:51,295 --> 00:08:52,878
(GIRLS LAUGHING)

107
00:08:53,213 --> 00:08:55,506
BOY: Looks like no one
wants you, Tommy.

108
00:08:56,133 --> 00:08:57,675
(BOYS LAUGHING)

109
00:09:02,889 --> 00:09:04,265
(YELLING)

110
00:09:04,349 --> 00:09:05,933
(GIRLS LAUGHING)

111
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:08,644
What's he doing?

112
00:09:08,770 --> 00:09:10,146
(YELLING CONTINUES)
It's his own fault.

113
00:09:10,230 --> 00:09:13,691
If he learnt to keep his cool,
they'd leave him alone.

114
00:09:13,859 --> 00:09:15,818
TOMMY: I hate you!
I hate you all!

115
00:09:15,902 --> 00:09:17,111
GIRL: Kath... No, don't!

116
00:09:18,905 --> 00:09:20,448
(YELLING STOPS)

117
00:09:20,574 --> 00:09:21,824
(SOBBING)

118
00:09:28,248 --> 00:09:30,041
You shouldn't have...
(GASPS)

119
00:09:50,062 --> 00:09:51,937
(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)

120
00:09:52,272 --> 00:09:54,106
Kathy H.

121
00:10:07,287 --> 00:10:08,954
What's this from?

122
00:10:09,623 --> 00:10:11,290
I don't remember.

123
00:10:11,458 --> 00:10:13,334
Doctor?

124
00:10:20,634 --> 00:10:22,802
It's nothing.
Just a bruise.

125
00:10:29,393 --> 00:10:31,102
LUCY: Hello.

126
00:10:31,603 --> 00:10:33,646
Kathy. Kathy H.

127
00:10:34,398 --> 00:10:36,816
Of course. Kathy H.

128
00:10:37,484 --> 00:10:39,026
Come in.

129
00:10:45,033 --> 00:10:46,575
So what can
I do for you?

130
00:10:47,994 --> 00:10:49,453
Well,

131
00:10:50,288 --> 00:10:53,332
I was wondering
what you said to Tommy.

132
00:10:54,459 --> 00:10:56,460
Out on the
playing fields.

133
00:10:56,545 --> 00:10:58,546
I see. Oh.

134
00:10:59,464 --> 00:11:01,132
So what did I say?

135
00:11:03,677 --> 00:11:06,053
I believe I was trying
to calm him down.

136
00:11:06,138 --> 00:11:08,013
Because he seemed upset.

137
00:11:08,515 --> 00:11:12,101
He explained
he was often teased
about sport and art,

138
00:11:12,185 --> 00:11:15,521
so I told him
he shouldn't get upset
about these things.

139
00:11:15,939 --> 00:11:19,567
The other children
are only teasing him
to get a reaction.

140
00:11:21,653 --> 00:11:24,363
And if it happens
that he's not particularly
good at sport or art,

141
00:11:24,448 --> 00:11:26,490
well, that doesn't matter.

142
00:11:27,284 --> 00:11:29,160
It's not so important.

143
00:11:34,875 --> 00:11:36,375
Thank you.

144
00:11:59,107 --> 00:12:00,566
Kathy.

145
00:12:03,570 --> 00:12:04,987
Aren't you sitting
with the girls?

146
00:12:05,071 --> 00:12:06,071
Well, I just checked

147
00:12:06,531 --> 00:12:08,783
and I'm pretty sure
I'm not sitting
with the girls.

148
00:12:08,867 --> 00:12:10,117
I'm sitting with you.

149
00:12:12,412 --> 00:12:13,954
I wanted to say

150
00:12:16,208 --> 00:12:17,875
I'm sorry.

151
00:12:19,252 --> 00:12:21,253
I didn't mean
to hit you.

152
00:12:22,422 --> 00:12:26,008
I mean, I wouldn't
want to hit any girl.

153
00:12:28,804 --> 00:12:30,471
But particularly
not you.

154
00:12:31,932 --> 00:12:34,725
Well, it was
an accident,
that's all.

155
00:12:36,937 --> 00:12:39,522
GERALDINE: Tommy,
finish those vegetables.

156
00:12:56,957 --> 00:12:59,375
I'm not gonna get angry
like that anymore.

157
00:12:59,459 --> 00:13:03,254
Because of what
Miss Lucy told you,
about being creative?

158
00:13:03,338 --> 00:13:04,922
How do you know
about that?

159
00:13:05,006 --> 00:13:06,048
I'm nosey.

160
00:13:09,469 --> 00:13:13,681
She can't actually
mean that being creative
isn't important, can she?

161
00:13:13,849 --> 00:13:16,058
I mean, what about
The Gallery?

162
00:13:16,142 --> 00:13:18,143
If being creative
isn't important,

163
00:13:18,228 --> 00:13:20,229
then why have
a gallery at all?

164
00:13:23,024 --> 00:13:24,483
I don't know.

165
00:13:29,489 --> 00:13:32,993
...by arching her back,
or perhaps with the aid
of a pillow,

166
00:13:33,911 --> 00:13:35,912
the woman can
arrange herself...

167
00:13:44,505 --> 00:13:46,548
(SINGING ON SCREEN)

168
00:13:51,429 --> 00:13:54,097
(SINGING) Smile

169
00:14:08,279 --> 00:14:09,696
Good morning, ma'am.

170
00:14:09,780 --> 00:14:10,947
Good morning.

171
00:14:11,032 --> 00:14:12,532
Can I help you?

172
00:14:12,617 --> 00:14:15,535
I wanted one tea,
with milk
and sugar in it.

173
00:14:16,287 --> 00:14:18,830
One tea. Right.

174
00:14:25,338 --> 00:14:26,880
LUCY: Tommy.

175
00:14:26,964 --> 00:14:29,549
Oh! One tea with
milk and sugar
in it, please.

176
00:14:29,884 --> 00:14:31,885
(CHILDREN CHUCKLING)
No, Tommy.

177
00:14:34,472 --> 00:14:36,681
I want to hear
your own order.

178
00:14:36,766 --> 00:14:38,433
Don't simply repeat
the one you've just heard.

179
00:14:39,226 --> 00:14:42,062
What other drinks
do they serve in cafes?

180
00:14:42,146 --> 00:14:43,480
Water.

181
00:14:43,564 --> 00:14:45,273
(CHILDREN LAUGHING)

182
00:14:45,566 --> 00:14:46,566
(LAUGHTER STOPS)

183
00:14:46,651 --> 00:14:47,651
(WHISPERING) Coffee.

184
00:14:47,735 --> 00:14:48,735
Coffee.

185
00:14:50,613 --> 00:14:51,905
LUCY: So, then.

186
00:14:52,782 --> 00:14:54,074
So, then,

187
00:14:55,242 --> 00:14:57,911
one coffee,
please, with milk
and sugar in it.

188
00:15:03,793 --> 00:15:05,126
(SOFTLY)
Thank you.

189
00:15:10,716 --> 00:15:12,175
(OWL HOOTING)

190
00:15:14,136 --> 00:15:16,096
RUTH: (WHISPERING)
I think Laura
likes Arthur.

191
00:15:16,931 --> 00:15:19,557
KATHY: (WHISPERING)
All the girls like
Arthur, don't they?

192
00:15:20,434 --> 00:15:21,935
Maybe.

193
00:15:23,437 --> 00:15:24,854
I'm not sure you do.

194
00:15:26,232 --> 00:15:28,358
He's not my type.

195
00:15:28,442 --> 00:15:31,695
I think Laura and Arthur
will be the first
in our year to have sex

196
00:15:31,779 --> 00:15:35,240
because, if Arthur wants to,
Laura wouldn't stop him.

197
00:15:35,950 --> 00:15:38,118
I'm sure you're right.

198
00:15:42,456 --> 00:15:43,915
Tommy's changed.

199
00:15:44,917 --> 00:15:46,334
Changed how?

200
00:15:47,628 --> 00:15:49,129
Just changed.

201
00:15:50,297 --> 00:15:51,673
That's all.

202
00:16:22,079 --> 00:16:24,664
EMILY:
What about this one
by Madeleine Kane?

203
00:16:25,041 --> 00:16:28,334
I think it shows more
than technical ability.

204
00:16:28,502 --> 00:16:31,046
It gives a real insight
into the child.

205
00:16:31,172 --> 00:16:34,215
Which is precisely
what Marie-Claude
wants for The Gallery.

206
00:16:34,300 --> 00:16:36,092
GERALDINE:
Yes, I agree.

207
00:16:39,847 --> 00:16:40,930
Mmm.

208
00:16:41,474 --> 00:16:43,349
EMILY: Put these
two together.

209
00:16:44,435 --> 00:16:46,061
(WHISPERING)
Madame is coming.

210
00:17:36,695 --> 00:17:38,404
EMILY: Good morning, students.

211
00:17:38,531 --> 00:17:41,533
CHILDREN:
Good morning,
Miss Emily.

212
00:17:42,910 --> 00:17:45,620
There are several
points of order today.

213
00:17:45,704 --> 00:17:49,457
The first is that our good
friend, Madame Marie-Claude,
is visiting us.

214
00:17:49,625 --> 00:17:53,837
She will, as usual,
be carefully inspecting
your artwork and poetry

215
00:17:54,004 --> 00:17:57,966
and selecting
the very best pieces
for inclusion in her Gallery.

216
00:17:58,384 --> 00:18:00,844
We will, of course,
extend to her every courtesy

217
00:18:00,928 --> 00:18:03,596
and make her feel
most welcome.

218
00:18:03,931 --> 00:18:07,517
Second point of order,
a piece of good news
for all students

219
00:18:07,601 --> 00:18:10,478
who have been diligent
in collecting tokens.

220
00:18:10,855 --> 00:18:13,690
After studies tomorrow,
there will be a sale.

221
00:18:13,774 --> 00:18:14,816
(CHILDREN CHATTERING
EXCITEDLY)

222
00:18:14,900 --> 00:18:17,277
The delivery
will arrive in the morning,

223
00:18:17,403 --> 00:18:19,737
and I was assured
by the van driver

224
00:18:19,822 --> 00:18:23,074
that the boxes will
contain a bumper crop.

225
00:18:23,534 --> 00:18:25,034
(ALL CHEERING)

226
00:18:31,292 --> 00:18:33,126
(SCHOOL BELL RINGING)

227
00:18:50,978 --> 00:18:53,396
(CHILDREN CHATTERING
INDISTINCTLY)

228
00:19:01,363 --> 00:19:03,031
(CLATTERS)

229
00:19:11,332 --> 00:19:12,749
(CHATTERING EXCITEDLY)

230
00:19:12,833 --> 00:19:14,959
Is it really
a bumper crop?

231
00:19:16,337 --> 00:19:19,756
I should say so.
Yeah, sweetheart.
It's a bumper crop.

232
00:19:20,049 --> 00:19:21,799
(CHILDREN CHEERING)

233
00:19:37,524 --> 00:19:39,442
(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)

234
00:20:24,571 --> 00:20:25,947
Hey.

235
00:20:27,157 --> 00:20:28,825
Aren't you buying?

236
00:20:29,201 --> 00:20:32,662
If you've already
spent all your tokens,
you can have some of mine.

237
00:20:32,913 --> 00:20:36,499
I have tokens.
I was just gonna wait
till the rush died down.

238
00:20:36,583 --> 00:20:39,335
I'm sure there'll be
something good left.

239
00:20:40,462 --> 00:20:42,422
What's the matter, Kath?

240
00:20:46,260 --> 00:20:47,802
Well, anyway,
it doesn't matter

241
00:20:47,886 --> 00:20:50,430
if you can't find
anything in the sale

242
00:20:50,514 --> 00:20:54,142
because I already
found you something.

243
00:20:56,854 --> 00:20:58,313
KATHY: A music tape.

244
00:20:58,397 --> 00:21:02,025
I don't know much about it,
if it's much good or anything.

245
00:21:06,238 --> 00:21:07,697
Thank you.

246
00:21:07,781 --> 00:21:10,616
(NEVER LET ME GO
PLAYING ON TAPE RECORDER)

247
00:21:13,454 --> 00:21:15,455
* Darling *

248
00:21:18,292 --> 00:21:29,136
* Hold me *

249
00:21:29,220 --> 00:21:30,804
* And never *

250
00:21:31,848 --> 00:21:35,642
* Never *

251
00:21:36,311 --> 00:21:39,938
* Let me go *

252
00:21:49,282 --> 00:21:51,533
* Darling *

253
00:21:53,661 --> 00:22:04,922
* Kiss me *

254
00:22:05,006 --> 00:22:06,882
* And never *

255
00:22:07,384 --> 00:22:11,512
* Never *

256
00:22:11,971 --> 00:22:16,683
* Let me go *

257
00:22:20,021 --> 00:22:21,688
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

258
00:22:47,799 --> 00:22:49,633
The problem is

259
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:54,012
you've been told
and not told.

260
00:22:54,848 --> 00:22:57,349
That's what I've seen
while I've been here.

261
00:22:58,685 --> 00:23:00,602
You've been told

262
00:23:00,895 --> 00:23:03,522
but none of you
really understand.

263
00:23:04,524 --> 00:23:08,652
So I've decided
I'll talk to you in a way
that you will understand.

264
00:23:12,323 --> 00:23:14,616
Do you know
what happens to children
when they grow up?

265
00:23:17,370 --> 00:23:21,081
No, you don't,
because nobody knows.

266
00:23:23,418 --> 00:23:25,961
They might grow up
to become actors,

267
00:23:26,963 --> 00:23:28,922
move to America.

268
00:23:30,675 --> 00:23:33,177
Or they might work
in supermarkets.

269
00:23:33,845 --> 00:23:35,596
Or teach in schools.

270
00:23:36,681 --> 00:23:41,518
They might become
sportsmen or bus conductors
or racing car drivers.

271
00:23:41,769 --> 00:23:44,188
They might do
almost anything.

272
00:23:46,524 --> 00:23:48,484
But with you
we do know.

273
00:23:51,613 --> 00:23:53,906
None of you
will go to America.

274
00:23:55,700 --> 00:23:58,660
None of you will work
in supermarkets.

275
00:24:00,914 --> 00:24:03,207
None of you
will do anything
except live the life

276
00:24:03,291 --> 00:24:05,501
that has already
been set out for you.

277
00:24:08,129 --> 00:24:10,214
You will
become adults,

278
00:24:11,966 --> 00:24:13,634
but only briefly.

279
00:24:17,096 --> 00:24:18,805
Before you are old,

280
00:24:20,391 --> 00:24:22,726
before you are
even middle-aged,

281
00:24:23,144 --> 00:24:26,480
you will start to donate
your vital organs.

282
00:24:28,983 --> 00:24:31,485
That's what you were
created to do.

283
00:24:33,988 --> 00:24:38,492
And sometime
around your third
or fourth donation,

284
00:24:39,577 --> 00:24:42,329
your short life
will be complete.

285
00:24:44,082 --> 00:24:48,418
You have to know
who you are
and what you are.

286
00:24:49,379 --> 00:24:52,548
It's the only way
you'll lead
decent lives.

287
00:25:43,808 --> 00:25:46,977
There will be only one
announcement this morning,

288
00:25:48,229 --> 00:25:49,771
which is that Miss Lucy,

289
00:25:49,856 --> 00:25:52,274
who was guardian
to the fourth-year students,

290
00:25:52,650 --> 00:25:55,027
is no longer
working at Hailsham.

291
00:25:55,111 --> 00:25:57,446
(CHILDREN MUTTERING
INDISTINCTLY)

292
00:25:58,948 --> 00:26:01,825
A replacement
will be found for her,
and in the meantime

293
00:26:01,909 --> 00:26:05,912
her classes
will be divided between
Miss Geraldine and myself.

294
00:26:08,041 --> 00:26:10,584
It is hard, is it not,

295
00:26:11,127 --> 00:26:15,130
to continue in the face
of deliberate subversion.

296
00:26:16,924 --> 00:26:20,927
There are those who seek
to thwart us, this is clear.

297
00:26:21,346 --> 00:26:25,015
And we are aware that the tide
is not with forward thinking.

298
00:26:25,099 --> 00:26:26,433
It never is.

299
00:26:27,602 --> 00:26:31,355
No, the tide is with
the entrenched mindset.

300
00:26:31,439 --> 00:26:34,066
With values that
are still unexamined.

301
00:26:36,819 --> 00:26:39,196
But I will not be coerced.

302
00:26:44,118 --> 00:26:46,286
I will not be coerced.

303
00:26:49,248 --> 00:26:51,625
And neither will Hailsham.

304
00:27:16,317 --> 00:27:18,568
(RUTH CHATTERING INDISTINCTLY)

305
00:27:56,149 --> 00:28:00,694
KATHY:
I didn't understand why,
after all her teasing,

306
00:28:01,696 --> 00:28:05,907
Ruth would decide
that Tommy was the boy
she liked most of all.

307
00:28:14,792 --> 00:28:18,170
They say girls are always mean
to the boys they like.

308
00:28:21,883 --> 00:28:24,551
So maybe Ruth
had liked him all along.

309
00:28:29,557 --> 00:28:32,434
Maybe I should have
teased him, too.

310
00:28:46,657 --> 00:28:49,075
I kept hoping
they would separate,

311
00:28:49,285 --> 00:28:53,038
like the other childhood
and teenage romances
at the school.

312
00:28:59,003 --> 00:29:00,545
They never did.

313
00:29:19,149 --> 00:29:20,817
(BIRDS TWITTERING)

314
00:29:34,957 --> 00:29:37,708
We were moved
from Hailsham at 18

315
00:29:37,793 --> 00:29:40,211
and sent to various
accommodations
around the country,

316
00:29:40,295 --> 00:29:43,548
to wait
until we were old enough
to start the donations.

317
00:29:46,385 --> 00:29:48,177
Tommy, Ruth and I

318
00:29:48,262 --> 00:29:52,223
were sent to a collection
of farm buildings
called The Cottages.

319
00:29:58,063 --> 00:29:59,730
During our stay there,
we were told

320
00:29:59,815 --> 00:30:03,442
that some of us
would be permitted
to volunteer as carers.

321
00:30:05,696 --> 00:30:09,824
But, apart from that,
we'd be left largely
to our own devices

322
00:30:09,908 --> 00:30:14,120
and even allowed
to take day trips into
the surrounding countryside.

323
00:30:19,167 --> 00:30:21,085
The Cottages were also
our first contact

324
00:30:21,169 --> 00:30:24,088
with those who had not
grown up at Hailsham.

325
00:30:24,339 --> 00:30:26,340
There were people
from White Mansion,

326
00:30:26,842 --> 00:30:31,387
Oakhill, Morningdale
and other homes
I'd never heard of.

327
00:30:34,308 --> 00:30:38,019
Everyone seemed wiser
and more worldly than us.

328
00:30:40,564 --> 00:30:43,524
None more so than
Rodney and Chrissie.

329
00:30:43,609 --> 00:30:46,319
They'd already been
at The Cottages
for over a year

330
00:30:46,403 --> 00:30:49,614
and would soon be leaving
for the completion centers.

331
00:30:49,698 --> 00:30:52,450
GIRL ON TV: Ricky?
I don't know what
you're talking about.

332
00:30:52,534 --> 00:30:54,410
Oh, I can
help you out there.

333
00:30:54,494 --> 00:30:58,414
He's talking about
blue eyes, blond hair,
cute dimples when he smiles.

334
00:30:58,498 --> 00:31:01,208
And biceps that
could crack walnuts.

335
00:31:01,293 --> 00:31:02,793
(ALL LAUGHING)

336
00:31:03,795 --> 00:31:05,713
BOY: Jennifer, get real,
I saw you with him.

337
00:31:05,797 --> 00:31:09,383
Jen and Ricky
sitting in a tree,
K-I-S-S-I-N-G.

338
00:31:10,218 --> 00:31:12,762
That is so not true.

339
00:31:15,057 --> 00:31:16,557
We weren't in a tree.

340
00:31:16,725 --> 00:31:18,476
(ALL LAUGH)

341
00:31:20,604 --> 00:31:22,730
(AUDIENCE APPLAUDING ON TV)

342
00:31:34,326 --> 00:31:35,743
Hey.

343
00:31:39,790 --> 00:31:41,582
Where you going?

344
00:31:42,250 --> 00:31:44,377
I thought
I'd go for a walk.

345
00:31:44,753 --> 00:31:48,756
Does that mean
you want to be alone?

346
00:31:59,351 --> 00:32:00,685
(CHUCKLES)

347
00:32:19,913 --> 00:32:21,914
I'm not even saying
that I'll apply
to be a carer.

348
00:32:21,999 --> 00:32:24,250
Just that several people
have said that
I'd be good at it.

349
00:32:24,584 --> 00:32:26,460
But then Laura went around
telling everyone

350
00:32:26,545 --> 00:32:29,046
that I'd been saying
I was the most likely
to get selected.

351
00:32:29,131 --> 00:32:30,214
That's so not true.

352
00:32:30,298 --> 00:32:31,757
So not true.

353
00:32:32,134 --> 00:32:33,718
Thank you.

354
00:32:33,802 --> 00:32:36,303
(INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC
PLAYING ON RADIO)

355
00:32:36,680 --> 00:32:38,639
RUTH: I'm getting
more plates.

356
00:32:40,308 --> 00:32:42,143
CHRISSIE: (LAUGHING)
Is there
anything left for us?

357
00:32:42,269 --> 00:32:44,270
(BOTH LAUGHING)
I don't know.

358
00:32:46,648 --> 00:32:48,024
CHRISSIE:
Perhaps not.

359
00:32:49,484 --> 00:32:51,736
Ruth, why do you
do that thing?

360
00:32:52,487 --> 00:32:53,863
Squeezing
Tommy's shoulder.

361
00:32:56,199 --> 00:32:57,825
I'm allowed
to touch Tommy,
aren't I?

362
00:32:57,909 --> 00:33:00,161
It's the way
you're touching him.

363
00:33:00,245 --> 00:33:02,663
You know what I mean.
It's copied from
that television show.

364
00:33:03,832 --> 00:33:04,832
That's so not...

365
00:33:04,916 --> 00:33:07,460
Don't tell me
"that's so not true."

366
00:33:09,463 --> 00:33:12,423
All that behavior,
that's not what people do
out there, in real life,

367
00:33:12,507 --> 00:33:13,758
if that's what
you were thinking.

368
00:33:13,842 --> 00:33:16,927
So what? Look,
it's no big deal.
A lot of us do it.

369
00:33:17,012 --> 00:33:19,096
What you mean is
Chrissie and Rod do it.

370
00:33:20,182 --> 00:33:22,016
Oh, Kathy.

371
00:33:24,770 --> 00:33:26,979
So that's what
this is about.

372
00:33:27,939 --> 00:33:29,023
It must be really
awful for you,

373
00:33:29,107 --> 00:33:30,566
surrounded by
all these couples.

374
00:33:31,151 --> 00:33:34,945
I never said that.
I just think it's daft.

375
00:33:35,655 --> 00:33:38,240
You copy them,
and they copy from
a television show.

376
00:33:38,325 --> 00:33:40,409
I'm right,
aren't I?

377
00:33:41,369 --> 00:33:45,748
You don't like the fact
that Tommy and I are friends
with Chrissie and Rod,

378
00:33:45,832 --> 00:33:47,708
whereas you hardly
speak to anyone.

379
00:33:50,712 --> 00:33:53,714
No. You're not right.

380
00:33:54,925 --> 00:33:56,801
"So" not right?

381
00:34:16,238 --> 00:34:18,072
Thank you, Mr. Keffers.

382
00:34:25,622 --> 00:34:27,039
(WHISTLING)

383
00:34:36,216 --> 00:34:37,842
(HEAVY BREATHING)

384
00:35:00,073 --> 00:35:01,740
(THUNDER RUMBLING)

385
00:35:24,639 --> 00:35:26,140
Hello, Tommy.

386
00:35:26,975 --> 00:35:28,726
Hello, Kath.

387
00:35:28,810 --> 00:35:30,144
(CHUCKLING)

388
00:35:32,189 --> 00:35:36,317
Well, don't just
hover in the doorway.
Come in. Join the fun.

389
00:35:37,944 --> 00:35:40,654
Didn't know you liked
that sort of stuff.

390
00:35:41,948 --> 00:35:43,699
You're very welcome to them
after I've finished.

391
00:35:44,159 --> 00:35:46,285
Oh, no,
it's just sex stuff.

392
00:35:46,828 --> 00:35:49,663
I expect I've seen
them all already anyway.

393
00:35:54,669 --> 00:35:56,086
Kathy, what are
you looking for?

394
00:35:56,838 --> 00:36:00,174
What do you mean?
I'm just looking
at dirty pictures.

395
00:36:01,509 --> 00:36:02,593
What, just for kicks?

396
00:36:02,677 --> 00:36:05,137
I suppose you could
say that, yes.

397
00:36:11,019 --> 00:36:14,480
If it's just for kicks,
then you don't do it
like that.

398
00:36:15,690 --> 00:36:18,317
You need to look
at each picture
more carefully.

399
00:36:18,526 --> 00:36:20,861
Nothing really happens
if you go that fast.

400
00:36:21,154 --> 00:36:23,489
How do you know
what works for girls?

401
00:36:25,700 --> 00:36:28,202
Kath, you're not
looking for kicks.

402
00:36:32,165 --> 00:36:35,960
Here, give it to Ruth.
See if it does
anything for her.

403
00:36:50,725 --> 00:36:55,729
Hi.

404
00:36:58,149 --> 00:37:00,526
I suppose you've heard
what Rod and Chrissie
have been saying.

405
00:37:01,569 --> 00:37:03,070
No, I haven't.

406
00:37:08,243 --> 00:37:10,286
Well, you know that
Rod and Chrissie

407
00:37:10,370 --> 00:37:11,830
have been thinking
of applying
to be carers,

408
00:37:11,914 --> 00:37:12,914
so they've been
learning how to drive.

409
00:37:12,999 --> 00:37:13,999
Yeah.

410
00:37:14,083 --> 00:37:18,753
Well, last weekend,
they went up to a town
on the Norfolk coast,

411
00:37:19,547 --> 00:37:22,257
and they think
they saw this person.

412
00:37:22,425 --> 00:37:23,842
What kind of person?

413
00:37:25,011 --> 00:37:26,553
A lady

414
00:37:27,722 --> 00:37:29,180
working in an office.

415
00:37:29,724 --> 00:37:30,890
And...

416
00:37:32,643 --> 00:37:34,269
What is it, Ruth?

417
00:37:36,105 --> 00:37:39,024
But they thought
this person
was a Possible.

418
00:37:41,402 --> 00:37:43,153
For me.

419
00:37:43,863 --> 00:37:45,196
They found
your Original?

420
00:37:45,281 --> 00:37:47,615
They aren't sure,
obviously,

421
00:37:47,700 --> 00:37:50,702
(EXCITEDLY) but Rodney
said that the resemblance
was really striking.

422
00:37:50,786 --> 00:37:53,079
Oh, my God. Ruth!
I know.

423
00:37:54,332 --> 00:37:58,001
They want to drive me
back up there so that
I can see for myself.

424
00:37:58,127 --> 00:38:00,378
Tommy will come, but...

425
00:38:00,713 --> 00:38:02,130
Do you want me to come?

426
00:38:05,634 --> 00:38:07,594
Ruth, of course.

427
00:38:17,813 --> 00:38:21,900
So, exactly how much
experience have you guys
had with the outside?

428
00:38:22,777 --> 00:38:23,818
Quite a lot.

429
00:38:23,903 --> 00:38:25,320
No, we haven't.

430
00:38:26,989 --> 00:38:30,075
We did a lot of role-playing
exercises at Hailsham.

431
00:38:30,159 --> 00:38:31,826
They don't count.

432
00:38:32,203 --> 00:38:35,455
RODNEY: Okay. Well,
don't feel scared.

433
00:38:35,581 --> 00:38:38,583
Okay? There's really...
There's nothing to it.

434
00:38:40,378 --> 00:38:42,003
We aren't scared.

435
00:38:42,838 --> 00:38:44,297
Good.

436
00:38:49,970 --> 00:38:51,930
(MUSIC PLAYING ON RADIO)

437
00:39:07,488 --> 00:39:08,947
I'll get sausage,
egg and chips.

438
00:39:13,494 --> 00:39:14,619
(SOFTLY) Sausage,
egg and chips, please.

439
00:39:14,703 --> 00:39:15,787
Yes, sausage,
egg and chips.

440
00:39:15,871 --> 00:39:17,914
Sausage, egg and chips,
me, too, please.

441
00:39:19,083 --> 00:39:22,252
Well, I suppose
I'd better get sausage,
egg and chips, too, then.

442
00:39:22,336 --> 00:39:26,464
Five sausage,
egg and chips. Drinks?

443
00:39:29,218 --> 00:39:30,718
Five Cokes, please.

444
00:39:37,309 --> 00:39:38,893
(GIRLS LAUGHING)

445
00:39:45,234 --> 00:39:47,527
CHRISSIE: It was good.
It was really good.

446
00:39:47,611 --> 00:39:48,987
(LAUGHING CONTINUES)

447
00:39:49,071 --> 00:39:50,280
What?
Sorry.

448
00:39:55,035 --> 00:39:59,372
You know, there's something
I've been wanting
to talk to you lot about.

449
00:40:00,708 --> 00:40:03,001
The problem is that
back there at The Cottages
it's impossible.

450
00:40:03,085 --> 00:40:05,420
Someone's always
listening in.

451
00:40:06,755 --> 00:40:08,173
CHRISSIE: So,

452
00:40:10,885 --> 00:40:13,178
someone was saying
that some Hailsham
students in the past

453
00:40:13,262 --> 00:40:15,930
have managed
to get a deferral.

454
00:40:16,098 --> 00:40:17,599
Apparently those
Hailsham students

455
00:40:17,683 --> 00:40:20,768
could have
their first donation
put back by three

456
00:40:21,479 --> 00:40:23,897
or even four years.

457
00:40:27,276 --> 00:40:29,277
As long as they qualified.

458
00:40:30,196 --> 00:40:32,405
If there was a boy
and a girl,

459
00:40:32,823 --> 00:40:34,824
and they were in love
with each other,

460
00:40:34,909 --> 00:40:38,745
really properly in love,
and they could prove it,

461
00:40:39,413 --> 00:40:42,332
then they would be given
a few years together

462
00:40:42,541 --> 00:40:44,959
before they began
their donations.

463
00:40:48,130 --> 00:40:50,381
Where did you
hear about this?

464
00:40:51,258 --> 00:40:52,634
When we were
at White Mansion.

465
00:40:52,760 --> 00:40:54,928
People there were
always going on about
this Hailsham couple.

466
00:40:55,596 --> 00:40:57,180
When the guy was
only a few weeks
from donation,

467
00:40:57,264 --> 00:41:00,767
he just went to see someone.
He applied,
and everything got sorted out.

468
00:41:01,519 --> 00:41:05,271
I suppose you lot
would know about
that sort of thing.

469
00:41:05,356 --> 00:41:09,150
Being from Hailsham,
you'd know how that
sort of thing works.

470
00:41:09,735 --> 00:41:12,153
Who is it
you go to?

471
00:41:13,155 --> 00:41:16,658
Who would we talk to
if we wanted to apply?

472
00:41:25,084 --> 00:41:26,960
To be honest,
I don't know what
you're talking about.

473
00:41:28,796 --> 00:41:30,505
Come on.

474
00:41:32,174 --> 00:41:33,508
Do you expect us
to believe that?

475
00:41:33,592 --> 00:41:36,427
Everybody knows
Hailsham is special.

476
00:41:37,012 --> 00:41:38,680
So what is it?

477
00:41:39,974 --> 00:41:42,559
Why keep these
things to yourself?

478
00:41:49,024 --> 00:41:51,693
There were lots of
stories at Hailsham.

479
00:41:52,653 --> 00:41:56,155
I don't think many of them
turned out to be true.

480
00:42:16,802 --> 00:42:18,303
(GULLS CAWING)

481
00:42:22,891 --> 00:42:24,809
It may be
down here.

482
00:42:34,903 --> 00:42:36,279
Yeah.

483
00:42:36,739 --> 00:42:38,072
That's it.

484
00:42:44,079 --> 00:42:45,955
(INDISTINCT CHATTERING)

485
00:43:04,516 --> 00:43:06,267
God, it must be her.

486
00:43:07,102 --> 00:43:09,562
Yeah, that's her.

487
00:43:09,730 --> 00:43:11,314
(INDISTINCT)

488
00:43:35,923 --> 00:43:37,548
RUTH:
It's not her.

489
00:43:39,176 --> 00:43:41,177
I'm not her.
No, you're not.

490
00:43:41,470 --> 00:43:42,887
It was really
close, though.

491
00:43:42,971 --> 00:43:45,556
Oh, shut up, Tommy!
It wasn't close.
It wasn't close at all.

492
00:43:45,641 --> 00:43:48,976
And I knew
it wasn't gonna be her
before we even got here!

493
00:43:49,478 --> 00:43:51,187
It was never
gonna be her.

494
00:43:51,313 --> 00:43:54,357
They never, ever model us
on people like that woman.

495
00:43:54,441 --> 00:43:56,067
Ruth, don't.
What?

496
00:43:56,694 --> 00:43:58,820
We all know it,
we just never say it.

497
00:44:00,114 --> 00:44:02,407
We are modeled on trash.

498
00:44:02,533 --> 00:44:06,494
Junkies, prostitutes,
winos, tramps.

499
00:44:07,121 --> 00:44:10,206
Convicts, maybe,
as long as they
aren't psychos.

500
00:44:11,333 --> 00:44:15,336
If you want to look
for Possibles, if you
want to do it properly,

501
00:44:17,506 --> 00:44:18,715
look in the gutter.

502
00:44:20,634 --> 00:44:22,635
That's where we came from.

503
00:44:50,205 --> 00:44:54,584
Hey.

504
00:44:59,673 --> 00:45:00,882
(SIGHS)

505
00:45:26,075 --> 00:45:28,076
We should go back.

506
00:46:21,923 --> 00:46:23,423
(DEVICE BEEPS)

507
00:46:44,487 --> 00:46:45,487
(DOOR CLOSING)

508
00:46:45,780 --> 00:46:47,906
(RUTH SHOUTING INDISTINCTLY)

509
00:46:49,909 --> 00:46:50,909
(THUDDING)

510
00:46:50,994 --> 00:46:53,620
RUTH: Don't tell me
to calm down!

511
00:46:54,581 --> 00:46:56,915
You walked down the pier
and you left me there.

512
00:46:57,000 --> 00:46:58,667
You knew
I was well upset.

513
00:46:58,751 --> 00:46:59,751
(THUDDING)

514
00:47:02,338 --> 00:47:03,547
(CHIRPS)

515
00:47:11,514 --> 00:47:12,514
BOY: Out here.

516
00:47:12,932 --> 00:47:14,474
(GIRL LAUGHING)

517
00:47:17,145 --> 00:47:18,645
(DOOR OPENING)

518
00:47:20,064 --> 00:47:21,565
(DOOR CLOSING)

519
00:47:27,655 --> 00:47:32,117
Chrissie and Rod
are pretty obsessed about
this rumor, aren't they?

520
00:47:32,201 --> 00:47:34,161
About the deferrals?
Mmm.

521
00:47:36,331 --> 00:47:39,374
I was thinking about it
again last night,

522
00:47:39,876 --> 00:47:41,835
and if this
rumor's true

523
00:47:43,046 --> 00:47:45,297
it might explain
a few things.

524
00:47:45,673 --> 00:47:47,132
Such as?
Well,

525
00:47:48,718 --> 00:47:50,135
The Gallery,
for instance.

526
00:47:50,219 --> 00:47:53,931
We never got to
the bottom of it,
what The Gallery was for.

527
00:47:54,307 --> 00:47:56,475
Pictures, poetry
and sculpture.

528
00:47:56,559 --> 00:47:58,101
Say something
about yourself.

529
00:47:58,269 --> 00:48:00,437
That's the whole point
about art, isn't it?

530
00:48:00,521 --> 00:48:03,774
It says what's inside of you.
It reveals your soul.

531
00:48:04,233 --> 00:48:05,817
Yeah. So?

532
00:48:07,946 --> 00:48:09,154
(SIGHS)

533
00:48:15,662 --> 00:48:19,748
Suppose for a second
that the rumor is true.

534
00:48:19,916 --> 00:48:23,043
That there is
a special arrangement
for Hailsham students,

535
00:48:23,127 --> 00:48:24,670
if they're in love.

536
00:48:25,672 --> 00:48:28,924
Well, there would
have to be
some kind of way

537
00:48:29,258 --> 00:48:33,261
to decide if couples
are telling the truth

538
00:48:33,680 --> 00:48:36,348
and not just lying
to put off their donations.

539
00:48:44,357 --> 00:48:46,858
That's what
The Gallery
could be for.

540
00:48:47,735 --> 00:48:50,112
In The Gallery
they have everything
about us they need to know.

541
00:48:50,196 --> 00:48:52,531
So if we say that
we're in love,

542
00:48:54,325 --> 00:48:56,660
they can look
into our souls
and they can see.

543
00:48:56,744 --> 00:48:59,454
They'll know
if it's real love
or if it's just a lie.

544
00:49:00,623 --> 00:49:02,666
That's a strange idea, Tommy.

545
00:49:04,252 --> 00:49:05,335
What?

546
00:49:07,505 --> 00:49:10,507
No, no. I don't think
that's any stranger
than any other idea.

547
00:49:10,591 --> 00:49:14,011
And didn't Miss Lucy say that
making art for The Gallery
wasn't even important?

548
00:49:14,095 --> 00:49:15,470
Yes.

549
00:49:17,265 --> 00:49:19,474
But look what
happened to her.

550
00:49:30,319 --> 00:49:32,529
Are you thinking
of applying?

551
00:49:33,322 --> 00:49:34,823
With Ruth?

552
00:49:38,202 --> 00:49:39,244
No.

553
00:49:40,538 --> 00:49:41,747
It wouldn't work.

554
00:49:43,666 --> 00:49:45,083
Why?

555
00:49:52,467 --> 00:49:54,009
Because

556
00:50:02,185 --> 00:50:03,685
you forget that

557
00:50:04,854 --> 00:50:07,439
you got lots of stuff
into The Gallery
over the years,

558
00:50:07,523 --> 00:50:10,984
and if I applied
they wouldn't have
anything to go on.

559
00:50:25,416 --> 00:50:27,584
It's all just rumors
and theories.

560
00:50:31,964 --> 00:50:33,423
Yeah, I know.

561
00:50:36,677 --> 00:50:38,178
Tommy.

562
00:50:56,239 --> 00:50:58,198
(BOTH BREATHING HEAVILY)

563
00:51:01,077 --> 00:51:02,285
(MOANS)

564
00:51:11,462 --> 00:51:13,421
(RUTH CONTINUES MOANING)

565
00:51:16,259 --> 00:51:17,884
(RUTH SQUEALING)

566
00:51:19,846 --> 00:51:21,054
(SIGHS)

567
00:51:25,101 --> 00:51:26,852
(NEVER LET ME GO
PLAYING ON HEADPHONES)

568
00:51:26,936 --> 00:51:38,488
* Kiss me *

569
00:51:38,573 --> 00:51:41,032
* And never *

570
00:51:41,117 --> 00:51:45,579
* Never *

571
00:51:45,663 --> 00:51:49,457
* Let me go *

572
00:51:57,967 --> 00:52:00,135
* Lock my heart *

573
00:52:02,597 --> 00:52:06,057
* Throw away the key *

574
00:52:07,101 --> 00:52:09,936
* Feel my love *

575
00:52:11,230 --> 00:52:12,689
(MUSIC STOPS)

576
00:52:28,831 --> 00:52:31,333
I know what
you think, Kathy.

577
00:52:34,587 --> 00:52:38,590
I know you think that you
and Tommy would have made
a more natural couple,

578
00:52:39,342 --> 00:52:40,842
and you believe

579
00:52:40,927 --> 00:52:44,387
that there's a chance
that Tommy and I
will split up some day.

580
00:52:45,848 --> 00:52:49,559
And when we do,
perhaps that will be
your chance with Tommy.

581
00:52:50,519 --> 00:52:52,854
Chance to do it
right this time.

582
00:53:00,239 --> 00:53:02,907
But you see,
the thing is, Kathy,

583
00:53:03,450 --> 00:53:06,869
although Tommy
really likes you
as a friend,

584
00:53:07,996 --> 00:53:10,706
he just doesn't
see you that way.

585
00:53:14,878 --> 00:53:17,964
He told me about
the porno magazines.

586
00:53:18,548 --> 00:53:19,924
(CHUCKLES)

587
00:53:20,759 --> 00:53:23,010
We had quite
a laugh about it.

588
00:53:25,973 --> 00:53:29,767
He doesn't understand
what you were doing.

589
00:53:31,853 --> 00:53:33,521
But I did.

590
00:53:37,234 --> 00:53:38,234
Oh!

591
00:53:58,088 --> 00:54:01,132
BOY: I said
I'll just be
five minutes.

592
00:54:06,305 --> 00:54:08,097
I want to be
a carer.

593
00:54:10,142 --> 00:54:11,517
It's you who
I apply to, isn't it?

594
00:54:11,601 --> 00:54:12,935
Yeah.

595
00:54:13,061 --> 00:54:15,062
I said that
I'm applying.

596
00:54:17,441 --> 00:54:19,442
I'll bring
the forms tomorrow,

597
00:54:19,526 --> 00:54:21,610
and that'll start
the process.

598
00:54:32,956 --> 00:54:36,125
KATHY: By the time
Ruth and Tommy
actually did separate,

599
00:54:36,209 --> 00:54:38,336
my carer training
had already begun.

600
00:54:40,547 --> 00:54:43,132
So I was hardly ever
at The Cottages.

601
00:55:08,408 --> 00:55:11,577
It had never occurred to me
that our lives,

602
00:55:12,079 --> 00:55:14,497
which had been
so closely interwoven,

603
00:55:17,417 --> 00:55:20,002
could unravel
with such speed.

604
00:55:34,768 --> 00:55:36,227
If I'd known,

605
00:55:37,187 --> 00:55:39,522
maybe I'd have kept
tighter hold of them

606
00:55:41,983 --> 00:55:45,194
and not let unseen tides
pull us apart.

607
00:56:12,722 --> 00:56:14,223
(ALARM BUZZING)

608
00:56:30,657 --> 00:56:32,324
Once I began working
with my donors,

609
00:56:32,409 --> 00:56:35,578
I didn't have much time
to dwell on Tommy or Ruth.

610
00:56:36,580 --> 00:56:38,289
Eventually,
so many years had passed

611
00:56:38,373 --> 00:56:41,125
I came to think
I would never
see them again.

612
00:56:41,293 --> 00:56:44,420
(INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC PLAYING)

613
00:56:48,425 --> 00:56:51,969
Many carers find
themselves just going
through the motions,

614
00:56:52,095 --> 00:56:56,265
waiting for the day
that they can stop
and become donors.

615
00:56:58,685 --> 00:57:00,186
(DEVICE BEEPS)

616
00:57:00,645 --> 00:57:04,190
But for the most part,
being a carer
has suited me fine.

617
00:57:06,276 --> 00:57:09,528
I quite enjoy
traveling across
the country,

618
00:57:09,613 --> 00:57:13,240
shuttling from
center to center,
hospital to hospital.

619
00:57:26,880 --> 00:57:28,339
KATHY: Here you are.

620
00:57:30,300 --> 00:57:31,800
Dark chocolate.

621
00:57:32,636 --> 00:57:35,971
If you knew how many
shops I had to go to
before I found those.

622
00:57:36,223 --> 00:57:39,600
I thought I wasn't
allowed to eat so soon
before the operation.

623
00:57:39,684 --> 00:57:42,853
Oh, you're not.
But I am.

624
00:57:42,938 --> 00:57:44,063
And after the operation,

625
00:57:44,147 --> 00:57:47,107
you can wolf down
the whole packet in one go.

626
00:57:52,656 --> 00:57:53,989
(SIGHING)

627
00:58:01,665 --> 00:58:04,750
KATHY: For me,
the real test
of being a carer

628
00:58:04,834 --> 00:58:07,962
is when a donor completes
earlier than expected.

629
00:58:08,672 --> 00:58:12,007
On the second
or even first donation.

630
00:58:12,425 --> 00:58:13,801
(SOFTLY)
Can I just have
a quick word?

631
00:58:13,885 --> 00:58:14,969
Okay.

632
00:58:16,221 --> 00:58:18,597
I was wondering who'd be
working on Hannah today.

633
00:58:18,682 --> 00:58:22,017
KATHY: I'd never say
I've become immune
to the completions.

634
00:58:22,978 --> 00:58:25,980
But they are something
I am able to live with.

635
00:58:30,360 --> 00:58:32,820
You're Hannah's carer,
aren't you?

636
00:58:33,738 --> 00:58:35,489
Hannah's carer?
Yes.

637
00:58:36,783 --> 00:58:40,411
I'm sorry, it's always hard,
but there were complications.

638
00:58:44,541 --> 00:58:46,000
You need me
to sign the release?

639
00:58:46,084 --> 00:58:47,293
Please.

640
00:58:49,504 --> 00:58:51,589
Are you going
to leave now?

641
00:58:51,673 --> 00:58:53,799
We can quite easily
provide you
with a bed tonight

642
00:58:53,883 --> 00:58:55,384
if you've got
a long drive.

643
00:58:55,510 --> 00:58:57,886
I can be back home
in under two hours.

644
00:58:57,971 --> 00:58:59,597
It's always better
to wake at home,
isn't it?

645
00:59:09,149 --> 00:59:10,816
Is that someone
you know?

646
00:59:10,942 --> 00:59:12,401
Yeah.

647
00:59:14,779 --> 00:59:16,697
Actually,
we grew up together.

648
00:59:17,073 --> 00:59:18,866
Oh.
How is she?

649
00:59:19,159 --> 00:59:21,035
Were you close?

650
00:59:21,161 --> 00:59:23,746
We haven't seen each other
now for almost 10 years.

651
00:59:23,830 --> 00:59:28,208
Well, Ruth isn't
as strong as we would
hope, at this stage.

652
00:59:30,420 --> 00:59:32,379
She's done
two donations.

653
00:59:32,922 --> 00:59:34,256
She has.

654
00:59:35,258 --> 00:59:36,759
You think
she'll complete
on the third?

655
00:59:37,636 --> 00:59:39,845
I think she wants
to complete.

656
00:59:40,764 --> 00:59:45,100
And, as you know,
when they want to complete,
they usually do.

657
01:00:43,493 --> 01:00:44,493
(DOOR OPENING)

658
01:00:56,924 --> 01:00:57,924
(PANTING SOFTLY)

659
01:01:02,179 --> 01:01:03,847
(SIGHS IN DISBELIEF)

660
01:01:04,306 --> 01:01:05,974
Kathy.

661
01:01:12,356 --> 01:01:13,857
(RUTH PANTING)

662
01:01:16,402 --> 01:01:19,362
RUTH: I've been
keeping tabs on you
over the years.

663
01:01:19,613 --> 01:01:21,156
And Tommy, too.

664
01:01:21,490 --> 01:01:23,450
KATHY: What do you
hear about Tommy?

665
01:01:23,534 --> 01:01:26,786
(SIGHS) That he's done
his second donation, too,

666
01:01:26,871 --> 01:01:29,706
and apparently
he's doing
very well on it.

667
01:01:30,374 --> 01:01:31,541
Apparently
he's in better shape

668
01:01:31,625 --> 01:01:34,085
than some after
their first donation.

669
01:01:34,295 --> 01:01:37,046
Good old Tommy.
I'm not surprised.

670
01:01:39,175 --> 01:01:41,134
Are you surprised at me?

671
01:01:43,137 --> 01:01:45,972
I expect I look
a bit broken, Kath.

672
01:01:47,057 --> 01:01:48,141
(SNICKERS)

673
01:01:48,225 --> 01:01:49,893
It's okay.

674
01:01:50,186 --> 01:01:53,229
I don't think
I'd want to survive
my third donation, anyway.

675
01:01:53,898 --> 01:01:56,024
You hear things,
don't you?

676
01:01:56,233 --> 01:01:58,193
What kind of things?

677
01:01:58,736 --> 01:02:00,528
Oh, you know.

678
01:02:03,741 --> 01:02:05,909
How, maybe,
after the fourth donation,

679
01:02:05,993 --> 01:02:08,787
even if you've
technically completed,

680
01:02:08,913 --> 01:02:12,081
you're still conscious
in some sort of way.

681
01:02:13,000 --> 01:02:17,170
And then you find out
that there are more
donations, plenty of them.

682
01:02:17,254 --> 01:02:21,466
No more recovery centers.
No more carers.

683
01:02:23,219 --> 01:02:25,261
Just watching
and waiting.

684
01:02:27,014 --> 01:02:29,349
Till they switch you off.

685
01:02:35,189 --> 01:02:37,607
I don't think
I fancy that.

686
01:02:41,612 --> 01:02:44,030
Is it the end
of the day already?

687
01:02:45,407 --> 01:02:47,158
Do you know,
it's funny,

688
01:02:47,243 --> 01:02:51,538
I think I've thought
about you every day
for the last few months.

689
01:02:51,622 --> 01:02:53,623
Hoping I'd see you again.

690
01:02:55,292 --> 01:02:58,127
Do you know,
I felt sure
that I would.

691
01:02:59,630 --> 01:03:04,717
Seemed impossible
that I'd complete without
seeing you one last time.

692
01:03:07,137 --> 01:03:09,639
It's not
the last time, Ruth.

693
01:03:10,641 --> 01:03:12,517
They offered me a bed.

694
01:03:12,601 --> 01:03:14,978
I was planning on
staying the night anyway.

695
01:03:15,187 --> 01:03:16,813
Well.

696
01:03:41,755 --> 01:03:44,257
RUTH: I always knew
you'd be a good carer.

697
01:03:44,717 --> 01:03:46,843
Your donors
are very lucky.

698
01:03:52,224 --> 01:03:55,310
Look, I was lying
awake last night

699
01:03:55,394 --> 01:03:58,354
and I suddenly had
an idea that we'd take
a trip together.

700
01:03:58,564 --> 01:04:00,356
Where to?

701
01:04:00,858 --> 01:04:03,735
I hadn't much thought
of where, just a trip.

702
01:04:08,115 --> 01:04:09,699
Although

703
01:04:11,285 --> 01:04:13,036
a place
did spring to mind.

704
01:04:13,120 --> 01:04:15,622
One of the boys
on the other floor
was talking about it.

705
01:04:15,706 --> 01:04:17,540
It's near the
Kingsfield Recovery Center.

706
01:04:18,042 --> 01:04:21,544
Is it the boat?
The one left
on the beach?

707
01:04:21,670 --> 01:04:23,254
You've heard
about it, too?

708
01:04:23,339 --> 01:04:25,882
I heard.
One of the carers
who worked there.

709
01:04:29,094 --> 01:04:30,845
And do you suppose,

710
01:04:31,889 --> 01:04:35,099
if we're driving
all that way,

711
01:04:35,851 --> 01:04:38,144
that we could
call in on Tommy?

712
01:04:40,272 --> 01:04:41,648
(CHUCKLING)

713
01:04:42,358 --> 01:04:45,318
Told you I'd been
keeping tabs on you.

714
01:04:47,029 --> 01:04:48,446
Have you seen him?

715
01:04:49,698 --> 01:04:51,032
No, not since
The Cottages.

716
01:04:52,076 --> 01:04:53,701
No, neither have I.

717
01:04:59,833 --> 01:05:01,334
RUTH: How will
we find him?

718
01:05:01,418 --> 01:05:04,087
KATHY: His carer said
he'd be waiting outside.

719
01:05:20,938 --> 01:05:22,438
Look, there he is.

720
01:05:29,321 --> 01:05:32,532
Do you think
he's recognized us
through the glass?

721
01:05:34,785 --> 01:05:36,619
No, no, no.

722
01:05:36,704 --> 01:05:38,705
Let's not get out.

723
01:05:55,389 --> 01:05:56,556
KATHY: Ruth's
in the car.

724
01:05:56,640 --> 01:05:57,807
Oh, right.

725
01:05:57,933 --> 01:05:59,308
Hey!

726
01:05:59,768 --> 01:06:01,144
Hi!

727
01:06:02,646 --> 01:06:03,980
Hi.
Hi.

728
01:06:04,314 --> 01:06:05,356
(CHUCKLES)

729
01:06:05,441 --> 01:06:06,482
How are you?

730
01:06:06,567 --> 01:06:07,650
RUTH: I'm good.

731
01:06:07,985 --> 01:06:09,193
Look at your hair.

732
01:06:09,278 --> 01:06:10,987
TOMMY: Oh, yeah.
My hair.

733
01:06:15,951 --> 01:06:17,285
(CHUCKLES)

734
01:06:19,413 --> 01:06:21,748
I can't quite believe
that I have you both here.

735
01:06:24,793 --> 01:06:26,377
Does it feel to you
that we're back
at Hailsham again?

736
01:06:26,462 --> 01:06:28,755
Like no time
has passed?

737
01:06:29,381 --> 01:06:30,465
No, it doesn't.

738
01:06:30,549 --> 01:06:31,841
TOMMY: No, it doesn't
at all, does it?

739
01:06:32,259 --> 01:06:35,011
It really doesn't at all.
It's really weird.

740
01:06:35,137 --> 01:06:36,763
But a good weird, I think.

741
01:06:41,643 --> 01:06:45,188
I don't suppose you both heard
that Hailsham was closed?

742
01:06:47,775 --> 01:06:51,027
The only schools left now,
you hear
they're like battery farms.

743
01:06:52,071 --> 01:06:53,696
Like Morningdale.

744
01:06:54,156 --> 01:06:57,200
I'm sure that's
an exaggeration, though.

745
01:06:59,328 --> 01:07:01,537
Are you sure
we're going
the right way?

746
01:07:17,721 --> 01:07:19,222
It's locked,
it looks like.

747
01:07:20,140 --> 01:07:21,641
No one said
anything about this.

748
01:07:21,725 --> 01:07:23,476
No one said anything
about a locked gate.

749
01:07:23,560 --> 01:07:26,395
Ruth, just relax.
It's okay.

750
01:07:35,072 --> 01:07:36,906
Can walk through here.

751
01:08:04,935 --> 01:08:05,977
Wow.

752
01:08:19,449 --> 01:08:21,576
Hey, come on.

753
01:08:42,055 --> 01:08:43,306
(PANTING)

754
01:08:52,192 --> 01:08:53,275
(GASPING)

755
01:08:53,359 --> 01:08:54,693
(COUGHING)

756
01:09:09,209 --> 01:09:12,336
TOMMY:
I wonder if that's what
Hailsham looks like now.

757
01:09:14,964 --> 01:09:17,925
Do you ever hear, Kath,
about any of the students
from Hailsham?

758
01:09:18,009 --> 01:09:19,927
Sometimes, yeah.

759
01:09:21,429 --> 01:09:26,391
I heard that
Amanda completed
on her first donation.

760
01:09:27,018 --> 01:09:30,103
I think that happens
more than they ever tell us.

761
01:09:30,188 --> 01:09:31,188
TOMMY: Hmm.

762
01:09:31,272 --> 01:09:35,067
Yeah, there was this
guy at my care center

763
01:09:35,193 --> 01:09:40,197
who was always really
scared of completing
on his first donation.

764
01:09:41,407 --> 01:09:43,200
But it was all fine.

765
01:09:43,284 --> 01:09:46,203
And he's just
come through
his third one now,

766
01:09:46,829 --> 01:09:49,706
and he's completely
all right.

767
01:09:54,837 --> 01:09:56,964
It's funny,
I don't think...

768
01:09:58,049 --> 01:10:00,133
Well, I know
I wouldn't have been
a very good carer,

769
01:10:00,218 --> 01:10:02,261
but in a way
I actually think
I'm quite a good donor.

770
01:10:02,345 --> 01:10:05,055
(SNICKERS) That's what
we're supposed to be,
isn't it?

771
01:10:17,610 --> 01:10:19,236
I'd like you
to forgive me.

772
01:10:21,864 --> 01:10:23,657
I don't expect
you to.

773
01:10:24,242 --> 01:10:25,367
Forgive you
for what?

774
01:10:32,667 --> 01:10:35,002
For keeping you
and Tommy apart.

775
01:10:38,881 --> 01:10:42,175
Should have been
you two together,
I always knew it.

776
01:10:42,260 --> 01:10:44,511
As far back
as I can remember.

777
01:10:46,514 --> 01:10:49,933
It wasn't just
because of the rumors
about deferrals.

778
01:10:54,397 --> 01:10:56,606
It was because
I was jealous.

779
01:10:59,193 --> 01:11:03,322
You had real love
and I didn't,

780
01:11:07,118 --> 01:11:10,454
and I didn't want to be
the one that was left alone.

781
01:11:21,591 --> 01:11:23,258
It's the worst thing
I ever did.

782
01:11:27,805 --> 01:11:30,140
And now I want
to put it right.

783
01:11:30,516 --> 01:11:32,225
I don't know
how you can
do that, Ruth.

784
01:11:32,727 --> 01:11:34,144
I can

785
01:11:35,646 --> 01:11:37,314
if you two
get a deferral.

786
01:11:38,316 --> 01:11:40,484
It's too late
for that, Ruth.

787
01:11:40,985 --> 01:11:43,820
Way too late.
It's stupid to even
think about it.

788
01:11:44,030 --> 01:11:48,200
It isn't too late.
Look, you'll see,
both of you.

789
01:11:48,951 --> 01:11:53,497
I wanted to do this trip
because I had something
that I wanted to give you.

790
01:11:54,957 --> 01:11:56,833
It's Madame's address.

791
01:11:59,754 --> 01:12:03,465
That's who you apply to.
That's who you have to
go and see.

792
01:12:06,260 --> 01:12:08,053
How did you
get this?

793
01:12:09,764 --> 01:12:13,558
It wasn't easy,
but I've had years
to think about what I did.

794
01:12:13,810 --> 01:12:18,188
And years to try and work out
a way to make it right.

795
01:13:03,234 --> 01:13:06,445
I started doing
them when we were
at The Cottages.

796
01:13:06,612 --> 01:13:09,114
The day after
we went to find
Ruth's Possible.

797
01:13:09,240 --> 01:13:12,159
I realized that
if I was ever going to
apply for a deferral,

798
01:13:12,243 --> 01:13:14,494
then I'd have to
show them something.

799
01:13:15,913 --> 01:13:18,915
And I've done
hundreds of them
over the years.

800
01:13:20,460 --> 01:13:22,127
They're wonderful.

801
01:13:22,920 --> 01:13:24,421
What? Really?

802
01:13:24,672 --> 01:13:26,089
Yeah.

803
01:13:27,675 --> 01:13:29,342
Well, thank you.

804
01:13:31,387 --> 01:13:32,971
(TOMMY CHUCKLES)

805
01:13:41,481 --> 01:13:44,608
I have never
showed anyone
them until now.

806
01:13:47,069 --> 01:13:49,154
They were my secret.

807
01:13:50,865 --> 01:13:53,617
Just like you
and those
porno magazines.

808
01:13:54,368 --> 01:13:56,036
You remember that?

809
01:13:56,454 --> 01:13:57,537
Yeah.

810
01:14:00,041 --> 01:14:01,958
Ruth said
you didn't understand
what I was doing.

811
01:14:02,043 --> 01:14:04,169
No, I don't think
she understood
what you were doing.

812
01:14:04,337 --> 01:14:06,338
She thought
you were looking
at the dirty pictures

813
01:14:06,422 --> 01:14:08,965
in order
to find out about sex

814
01:14:09,342 --> 01:14:10,634
(SIGHING)

815
01:14:11,052 --> 01:14:14,679
and to find out what
you were missing out
on or something.

816
01:14:18,184 --> 01:14:19,726
I knew.

817
01:14:21,395 --> 01:14:24,523
I knew you were looking
for your Original.

818
01:14:27,193 --> 01:14:30,987
I used to have
these huge urges
to have sex sometimes.

819
01:14:31,989 --> 01:14:34,533
And sometimes
they'd be so powerful,

820
01:14:35,076 --> 01:14:36,993
I just thought
I'd do it with anyone.

821
01:14:39,497 --> 01:14:42,332
So I thought that
had to tell me something.

822
01:14:42,458 --> 01:14:45,252
Something about
the kind of person
I was modeled on.

823
01:14:47,672 --> 01:14:49,339
It's stupid,

824
01:14:50,925 --> 01:14:54,010
but I guessed the magazines
would be the most likely
place I'd find her.

825
01:14:56,180 --> 01:14:57,931
You know those urges
are natural, don't you?

826
01:14:58,015 --> 01:15:01,560
You know all of us had them.
You know that now, right?

827
01:15:15,283 --> 01:15:17,033
KATHY:
"From Basrah we sailed,

828
01:15:18,452 --> 01:15:22,205
"day after day,
night after night,
over the sea,

829
01:15:23,332 --> 01:15:27,210
"visiting island after island
and land after land,

830
01:15:28,546 --> 01:15:31,590
"selling or bartering
our goods at each.

831
01:15:35,177 --> 01:15:39,639
"One day,
after some weeks of sailing
out of sight of land,

832
01:15:39,724 --> 01:15:42,434
"we saw an island in the sea
with such fair greenery

833
01:15:42,518 --> 01:15:45,729
"that it appeared
like one of the
gardens of Eden.

834
01:15:46,314 --> 01:15:49,733
"At once
the captain made towards
this delectable land

835
01:15:49,817 --> 01:15:53,820
"and, when the anchor
had been cast
and the ladder lowered,

836
01:15:53,904 --> 01:15:56,740
"allowed his passengers
to disembark."

837
01:16:03,372 --> 01:16:05,081
(WHISPERS) Don't stop.

838
01:18:30,937 --> 01:18:35,816
KATHY: Ruth got it right.
Right street,
right door, everything.

839
01:18:40,322 --> 01:18:43,866
We have to decide
which of my drawings
we're gonna take.

840
01:18:47,287 --> 01:18:49,914
Just take the best ones.
Maybe six or seven of them.

841
01:18:49,998 --> 01:18:51,999
You have to help me.
Yeah, I know.

842
01:18:53,835 --> 01:18:55,377
(BOTH LAUGHING)

843
01:18:58,340 --> 01:19:01,175
So, we'll just go there.

844
01:19:01,259 --> 01:19:02,301
Mmm.

845
01:19:02,385 --> 01:19:04,053
Next week.

846
01:19:04,137 --> 01:19:06,680
I'll take you out
for lab tests, I'll sign
you out for the whole day

847
01:19:06,765 --> 01:19:08,474
and we'll visit Madame
on the way back.

848
01:19:25,158 --> 01:19:27,284
KATHY: We're going to do it.

849
01:19:29,579 --> 01:19:31,622
I thought you should know.

850
01:19:34,626 --> 01:19:36,502
You're going to apply?

851
01:19:39,256 --> 01:19:40,631
Yes.

852
01:19:46,388 --> 01:19:47,805
Good.

853
01:20:01,319 --> 01:20:02,820
(DOOR OPENING)

854
01:20:05,240 --> 01:20:06,740
(DOOR CLOSING)

855
01:20:06,867 --> 01:20:08,117
(SIGHS)

856
01:20:10,871 --> 01:20:12,496
(MONITOR BEEPING)

857
01:20:22,674 --> 01:20:24,675
(MONITOR BEEPING RAPIDLY)

858
01:20:25,844 --> 01:20:27,219
(MONITOR FLATLINING)

859
01:21:10,347 --> 01:21:11,847
(GULLS CAWING)

860
01:21:50,929 --> 01:21:52,805
Madame?

861
01:21:58,103 --> 01:21:59,520
Sorry.

862
01:22:00,397 --> 01:22:02,606
We didn't mean
to startle you.

863
01:22:03,274 --> 01:22:05,025
We were at Hailsham.

864
01:22:09,781 --> 01:22:12,116
I'm Kathy.
This is Tommy.

865
01:22:14,619 --> 01:22:16,954
We're not here to
give you any trouble.

866
01:22:20,417 --> 01:22:21,875
From Hailsham?

867
01:22:21,960 --> 01:22:23,502
TOMMY: We just came
to talk to you.

868
01:22:25,380 --> 01:22:27,631
And I brought you
some things.

869
01:22:28,633 --> 01:22:31,510
Some things
you might like
for your Gallery.

870
01:22:33,680 --> 01:22:35,264
Come inside.

871
01:22:53,658 --> 01:22:55,993
(SOFT INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC
PLAYING)

872
01:23:03,001 --> 01:23:04,501
(DOOR OPENING)

873
01:23:05,837 --> 01:23:07,337
(DOOR CLOSING)

874
01:23:10,759 --> 01:23:12,760
Please, wait here.

875
01:23:30,862 --> 01:23:32,654
Look, it's Hailsham.

876
01:23:34,365 --> 01:23:36,533
Kathy, it's Hailsham.

877
01:23:38,495 --> 01:23:41,205
KATHY: That's exactly
how I remember it.

878
01:23:48,296 --> 01:23:49,922
Please, sit down.

879
01:24:05,230 --> 01:24:06,605
So...

880
01:24:09,818 --> 01:24:11,318
We're in love.

881
01:24:12,112 --> 01:24:14,655
And it's true love.
It's verifiable.

882
01:24:15,740 --> 01:24:17,741
Verifiable. I see.

883
01:24:23,790 --> 01:24:25,290
Go on.

884
01:24:25,542 --> 01:24:26,959
Well,

885
01:24:28,628 --> 01:24:31,255
we'd heard about
the deferrals.

886
01:24:33,466 --> 01:24:36,219
And we'd worked out
the purpose
of The Gallery.

887
01:24:37,596 --> 01:24:39,347
Tell me the purpose.

888
01:24:41,142 --> 01:24:44,227
To use our art
from Hailsham

889
01:24:44,436 --> 01:24:46,771
to look into
our souls,

890
01:24:47,815 --> 01:24:51,234
which would verify
that we deserved
a deferral.

891
01:24:51,944 --> 01:24:55,321
(CHUCKLING)
But the problem is

892
01:24:55,406 --> 01:24:58,283
I was a little bit
mixed up back then

893
01:24:58,367 --> 01:25:02,579
and I didn't
really do any art,

894
01:25:04,248 --> 01:25:06,583
so you never took
anything of mine.

895
01:25:06,667 --> 01:25:10,128
I know, I know that
that is my fault

896
01:25:10,212 --> 01:25:13,423
and it is probably
much too late,

897
01:25:13,507 --> 01:25:15,508
but I brought
some stuff
with me today.

898
01:25:21,473 --> 01:25:24,559
This is a book
of stuff and...

899
01:25:36,655 --> 01:25:38,698
Other stuff here.

900
01:25:42,244 --> 01:25:43,411
(TOMMY MUMBLING)

901
01:25:43,495 --> 01:25:46,623
Some of them I've done
quite recently,

902
01:25:47,666 --> 01:25:49,000
and others

903
01:25:50,961 --> 01:25:53,838
(STUTTERING) were done
several years ago,
so there's a real spread.

904
01:25:55,132 --> 01:25:58,426
You already have
a load of stuff
from Kath already.

905
01:25:58,510 --> 01:26:01,554
She got loads of stuff
into The Gallery over
the years, didn't...

906
01:26:01,680 --> 01:26:03,348
I'm sorry.

907
01:26:06,685 --> 01:26:08,645
I never know
what to do.

908
01:26:12,566 --> 01:26:14,192
(FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING)

909
01:26:15,486 --> 01:26:17,362
I'll take it
from here.

910
01:26:23,244 --> 01:26:24,911
EMILY:
Thank you, George.

911
01:26:29,375 --> 01:26:31,793
Kathy H and Tommy D.

912
01:26:31,877 --> 01:26:32,877
(DOOR CLOSING)

913
01:26:33,545 --> 01:26:35,505
I remember you both.

914
01:26:36,048 --> 01:26:39,175
Kathy, a bright girl,
and so creative.

915
01:26:39,468 --> 01:26:42,762
And Tommy, a big heart
and terrible rages.

916
01:26:47,017 --> 01:26:48,726
You have to understand,

917
01:26:49,728 --> 01:26:54,315
Hailsham was
the last place to consider
the ethics of donation.

918
01:26:54,733 --> 01:26:58,111
We used your art to show
what you were capable of.

919
01:26:58,779 --> 01:27:01,447
To show that donor children
are all but human.

920
01:27:03,284 --> 01:27:07,245
But we were providing
an answer to a question
no one was asking.

921
01:27:08,831 --> 01:27:11,040
If you ask people
to return to darkness,

922
01:27:11,125 --> 01:27:15,628
the days of lung cancer,
breast cancer,
motor neurone disease,

923
01:27:16,588 --> 01:27:18,464
they'll simply say no.

924
01:27:21,844 --> 01:27:25,138
We used to get
two or three couples
like you a year.

925
01:27:26,765 --> 01:27:28,683
Not so much these days.

926
01:27:28,767 --> 01:27:29,767
(CHUCKLES)

927
01:27:30,185 --> 01:27:32,687
You're the first
for quite a while.

928
01:27:36,817 --> 01:27:39,110
To apply
for a deferral?

929
01:27:47,536 --> 01:27:49,954
There are
no deferrals, Tommy.

930
01:27:54,960 --> 01:27:59,172
There are no deferrals.
And there never have been.

931
01:28:09,641 --> 01:28:13,394
We didn't have
The Gallery in order
to look into your souls.

932
01:28:13,812 --> 01:28:17,023
We had The Gallery
to see if you had
souls at all.

933
01:28:30,079 --> 01:28:31,746
Do you understand?

934
01:28:42,633 --> 01:28:44,008
Yes.

935
01:28:50,849 --> 01:28:53,684
Your drawings
are very good.

936
01:28:55,020 --> 01:28:57,522
If you want,
I'll keep them.

937
01:29:59,960 --> 01:30:02,086
Thank you
for talking to us.

938
01:30:05,924 --> 01:30:07,675
You poor creatures.

939
01:30:09,470 --> 01:30:11,429
I wish I could help you.

940
01:31:11,365 --> 01:31:13,991
Sorry, can we stop
for a second?
I need to get out.

941
01:31:40,018 --> 01:31:41,561
(TOMMY YELLING)

942
01:33:56,948 --> 01:33:58,323
(INAUDIBLE)

943
01:34:21,806 --> 01:34:24,391
KATHY: It's been two weeks
since I lost him.

944
01:34:55,006 --> 01:34:57,215
I've been given my notice now.

945
01:35:01,053 --> 01:35:03,722
My first donation
is in a month's time.

946
01:35:12,398 --> 01:35:15,650
I come here and imagine
that this is the spot

947
01:35:15,735 --> 01:35:19,654
where
everything I've lost since
my childhood has washed up.

948
01:35:23,618 --> 01:35:26,953
I tell myself
if that were true,

949
01:35:27,789 --> 01:35:30,123
and I waited long enough,

950
01:35:31,209 --> 01:35:35,086
then a tiny figure
would appear on the horizon
across the field

951
01:35:35,254 --> 01:35:39,216
and gradually get larger
until I'd see it was Tommy.

952
01:35:42,261 --> 01:35:43,845
He'd wave

953
01:35:44,305 --> 01:35:46,181
and maybe call.

954
01:35:48,518 --> 01:35:51,561
I don't let the fantasy
go beyond that.

955
01:35:51,771 --> 01:35:53,104
I can't let it.

956
01:35:55,233 --> 01:35:59,236
I remind myself
I was lucky to have had
any time with him at all.

957
01:36:03,783 --> 01:36:06,034
What I'm not sure about

958
01:35:50,914 --> 01:35:55,500
is if our lives have been
so different from the lives
of the people we save.

959
01:36:14,085 --> 01:36:16,002
We all complete.

960
01:36:18,464 --> 01:36:22,175
Maybe none of us
really understand
what we've lived through

961
01:36:24,136 --> 01:36:26,680
or feel we've had
enough time.
